# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
NOTES = {
0:'五脏六腑 is the Chinese medical inventory of organs. Spelling it out in English turns a throwaway phrase into a lecture, so I let it mean what it means in use, the internal organs. Both halves of the paradox have to survive. Han Dian has 空窍 as a real anatomical word, the openings of the body, quoting Han Feizi, so aperture sits at the right register and is not a coinage.',
1:'Both names are real internal alchemy vocabulary, and my first note had the wrong sense of 华池. Han Dian leads with the mouth, the hollow under the tongue, which is a genuine sense but not the one operating here. The neidan diagram tradition gives the other: 脐后肾前有一穴叫炎海，里面有华池，古人称为下田，乃藏精之所, behind the navel and in front of the kidneys is a cavity called the Qi Sea, and inside it the Flowery Pool, which the ancients called the lower field, where essence is stored. That is the exact location already published for the aperture in Chapter 5, below the navel and between the kidneys. 紫府 works the same way: Baidu Baike gives one reading as the dwelling of immortals and the other as 人身体内修炼的关窍, the cultivation aperture inside the body, and neidan glossaries list it among the standing aliases of the upper dantian. So the author is not decorating. He is offering two established names for a cultivation cavity, one usually upper and one usually lower. They stay capitalised.',
2:'I started with congealed and changed it. Han Dian defines 凝结 as gas turning to liquid or liquid turning to solid, English condensation, with a second sense of gathering. Congeal pulls hard toward solidity, which is wrong for light and wrong for a sea. Condensed covers both, and I am holding it throughout. The sentence also read as a contradiction, bursting and condensing in one breath, so I have recast it as a sequence: what the Hope Gu became.',
3:'支撑 is to prop or brace. Held it up was wrong, because a sphere has no up. Held together is what a brace against collapse actually does.',
5:"浓稠 has no headword of its own in Han Dian, but the characters are 浓, dense, and 稠, thick in the sense of not flowing freely. Thick in English is ambiguous, it can mean deep or opaque. Viscous is the actual property, and it matters, because the reader needs to know this is not water. 铜 alone is copper, but the next line names the substance bronze, so I take the sheen as bronze to keep the two lines agreeing. On the green and the bronze looking like a contradiction, they are not. 青铜 is named for its green. Han Dian gives bronze as 呈青灰色, greenish grey, and Chinese Wikipedia says outright 因为其氧化物颜色青灰，故名青铜, named for the greenish grey of its oxide. The Cantonese entry is blunter: fresh bronze is 古铜色, not green at all, and it is called 青铜 only because it corrodes to blue green 铜绿. And Han Dian's 碧 is 青绿色的玉石, green jade, with 碧水 for green water. So jade green plus a copper sheen is a description of patinated bronze, which is exactly what the next line names it. He describes the colour, then names the metal.",
6:'真元 is not a coinage. Han Dian gives it as the root vital energy of the body, with a Tang citation, and ties it to the original qi stored by the kidneys. Primeval essence keeps it locked to 元海, primeval sea, which is the same 元.',
7:'四成四 is forty-four percent, and it attaches to 高度, height. So this is a fill level in a sphere, not a volume.',
8:'精气神 is a fixed Daoist triad. Han Dian glosses it flatly as vitality, but separates the three underneath: 精 the refined substance in tangible form, 气 the same in intangible form, 神 the living activity of both. Collapsing it to vitality throws away a structure the book will use again, so I keep three words.',
10:'汇入 is the word for tributaries joining a river. The image is an inflow stopping, which is why I avoid entered or poured.',
11:'收起心神 is pulling attention back from the inward look to the outside world. It closes the interior passage that opened the chapter.',
12:'Han Dian defines 淡然 as 漫不经心、毫不在意的样子, offhand and not minding in the slightest, English indifferently, coldly. A second gloss adds tranquil and calm. Both readings exist, but the core is not caring. Calm on its own is too neutral, it could just mean composed. Indifferent is the actual weight, and it is the point of the chapter.',
13:"My first justification for keeping the number was bad. Saying the clan's hopes are the chapter's subject explains nothing about why a numeral should survive. The real reason is narrower. 万分之一 is a stock intensifier, the same move as one in a million, meaning vanishingly small rather than a real probability. English has that move available, so nothing is lost by keeping a numeral, while the faintest hope quietly drops a figure the author did write.",
14:None,
15:'反应 alone is to react. But the verb here is 反应过来, and the 过来 does the work, marking arrival at a state. The unit means the penny drops. So reacted would be wrong, and understood is right but flat, since it loses the slowness. Caught on carries both: they got there, and they got there late.',
16:'嗡 is a drone or buzz, and it sits inside the sentence rather than standing alone, so it does not break the rule I set about isolated sound words. 炸开了锅 is set, the pot boils over, used of a crowd going up in noise. Rendering the pot literally would be gibberish.',
17:'Twenty odd has turned up in an earlier chapter. Numbers are exactly where a translator smooths without noticing, so the earlier chapters need a sweep for this.',
20:'Han Dian gives 轩然大波 two senses, the literal high surging wave out of a Han Yu poem, and the figurative furore. The primeval sea is sixteen lines above, and repeated wave imagery is something I have ruled out for this translation. Keeping the wave would read as if the sea had followed him out of his own body. Same reasoning at L54, where 一阵阵 becomes burst after burst rather than wave after wave, with the added point that there is a literal river in shot.',
21:'Dropping elder everywhere was sloppy of me. It depends on position. 哥哥 as direct address is what a boy actually calls his brother, and English speakers say Brother, not Elder Brother, out loud. In narration the seniority is information, and English carries it without strain. So Brother when he speaks to him, his elder brother when the narrator refers to him.',
23:"舅父 is specifically the mother's brother, 舅母 his wife. English collapses this. Writing maternal uncle and his wife is accurate and unreadable, so I take the loss and record it here.",
25:'可恶 is disgust at a situation, not swearing at a person. The hidden clench matters, he is performing composure in public. 溢于言表 is in Han Dian as feeling overflowing through and past speech, English beyond words. I had this as an absolute phrase hanging off a comma, grammatical but stiff. A full stop and a finite verb read better.',
26:'Sighed long at the heavens was me translating the characters and calling it English. 仰天长叹 is a physical posture, the head goes back, then the sigh. Describing the posture gets the same image without the incense-burning tone.',
27:'Inverting this into could the test be in error gained nothing. 会不会 is a floated possibility, and the straight question carries that better.',
28:'准确 is accurate, hitting the mark. Precise is a different property, about fineness and repeatability, and it is not what the character says.',
29:'才情 is talent plus the temperament that displays it. Wider than talent, but gifts is vaguer. I take the loss.',
30:'悟性 is the capacity to grasp and awaken to things, a cultivation word. Comprehension is the nearest plain English. The five-item list stays a list, the elder is being systematic and slightly pompous.',
31:"一代不如一代 is set, and Han Dian's example is the famous Dream of the Red Chamber line about a great house whose sons and grandsons decline. Exactly the note these elders are striking.",
32:'The same marker as in Chapter 5, not missing text. Three spaced asterisks is the workhorse scene break in published English fiction: unambiguous, and it survives reflow on a phone, where a bare blank line vanishes at the foot of a screen.',
33:None,
34:'上百 is upward of a hundred, so more than a hundred, not a hundred. 清晰 for sight and 敏锐 for perception are deliberately paired, so I keep two different English words. Gazes as a plural noun was the weak part, and 投来 is cast toward, so eyes fixed on him carries it and gives L35 its antecedent.',
35:"Six items on the same 有 frame. The repetition is the effect. Smoothing it into one English series would kill it. 幸灾乐祸 is in Han Dian out of the Zuo Zhuan, taking pleasure in another's disaster, and the dictionary itself offers schadenfreude, but a German loanword would stick out of this list, so plain English it is.",
37:"失魂落魄 in Han Dian is the souls departing, used of someone shattered and running on autopilot. 搀扶 is specifically supporting someone by the arm, so the thing nobody did is physical, not emotional. That is colder, and better. This is also the hinge of the chapter, and it runs on a reversal. In the former life it was Fang Yuan who fell, 自己, and 却没有一个人攑扶自己, not one person put out a hand. In this life Fang Zheng falls and Fang Yuan is the hand. Note the cold detail that survives the reversal: nobody 攑扶'd Fang Yuan, and Fang Yuan does not 攑扶 Fang Zheng either. He 提s him, grips him by the collar the way one lifts a bag.",
38:'凌迟 is not generic cutting. Han Dian has it as the judicial execution by slow slicing, quoting the Song History on severing the limbs first and the throat last, and noting the popular name, a thousand knives and ten thousand cuts. So the English has to be incremental. I am not importing an image here, it is the verb on the page, and it is the most protracted killing the language had available.',
40:'A fragment standing as its own paragraph, and the hinge of the whole chapter. Completing it into a sentence would wreck the turn.',
41:"First application of the rule I am now using for 心. Han Dian's entry notes that the ancients treated the heart as the organ of thought, so the word stands in for the mind as readily as for feeling. Here 心 is his inner state generally, and English already says a person is calm without routing it through an organ. Keeping heart would also be the third heart in a short stretch.",
42:"困境 is the same word settled as predicament in Chapter 5, and this is a direct callback to the Ren Zu legend already published, so the wording has to match what is live. Heart stays here, because handing the heart over is the legend's own image, not a stock phrase.",
45:"Changed to one. 自己 is oneself, and it does not commit to a person. The passage is sliding out of Fang Yuan's private thought and into maxim, which is exactly why the next two lines read like something carved on a wall. Switching to one makes that drift visible instead of hiding it behind an I. L44 is still his own thought, so the handover happens right here.",
46:"五百年 stays explicit, per the standing rule on that repetition. This is one of the three places 精彩 appears, and I have settled on interesting across all three. 精彩 does mean splendid or brilliant, so interesting is the looser word. But the road Fang Zheng is about to walk is not exciting in any ordinary sense. It is interesting in the specific sense of growth, watched from outside by someone who already knows how it goes. That reading also makes the echo work harder than mine did: here the word is Fang Yuan's sincere creed about his own life, and at L60 he hands the same word to his brother with a savouring smile. Same word, sincere and then sinister. If it ever reads too thin here, brilliance restores the weight at the cost of the link.",
47:"The one that matters. This is not the author's own sentence, it is a rewrite of a proverb every Chinese reader knows, 走自己的路，让别人说去吧, go your own way and let other people talk. He swaps talk for be disappointed and dislike it, so the borrowed motto lands precisely on Fang Yuan's situation. The English therefore has to sound like the familiar saying with a substitution made, which is why go your own way stays verbatim and up front. The proverb reaches Chinese through Marx, who closed the 1867 preface to Capital with Segui il tuo corso, e lascia dir le genti, itself an alteration of a line spoken by Virgil, not Dante, in Purgatorio V.",
49:'却 sets up the joke that follows. Short is right.',
50:'溶洞 is specifically a limestone solution cave, the standard word for the karst country of south China, which is where this clan lives. 嗡嗡 picks up the 嗡 from L16.',
51:'恰巧 means it so happened, by coincidence. The narrator is not saying he fell in the river, he is saying that of all the places he could have fallen, he managed the river. The word exists to point at the coincidence and enjoy it. As luck would have it does that job. If it reads too arch, just happened to is flatter and also fine.',
52:'哄堂大笑 in Han Dian is a whole room laughing at the same moment. I first reached for the metonym, the cavern laughing, which English does not really grant to a hole in the ground. Putting the laughter itself as the subject keeps the sense of the whole enclosure going up at once, without the cave acquiring a mouth.',
53:"Nothing states disgust toward Fang Yuan outright, so the 也, also, is doing something sly. It points back to L25, where the man clenched his fists and called the result detestable. Chinese lets 也 reach back to an attitude the reader is expected to have inferred, without that attitude ever being named. English as well does the same reaching, so I keep it: the sneaky part is the author's, not mine. Making it explicit would spell out a joke the original leaves half-said.",
54:'一阵阵 is burst after burst. No waves, for the reasons at L20, and there is a literal river here. 心中 dropped, per the rule on 心, since this is agitation and English puts that in the person. 奈何 is a lament, so the cause belongs after the failure rather than before it.',
55:'My first attempt, felt a great strength seize hold of him, was limp, and the literal reading was better because it kept the verb. 拽 is a hard yank, not a gentle seizing, and it sets up Fang Yuan hauling him by the collar in the next line. Put the yank back and the sentence works. On whether he was drowning: 脑袋终于脱离了水面, his head finally left the surface, so yes, he was face down in it, and 终于 says it had lasted.',
56:'提 is to carry by a grip from above, the way one lifts a bag. Faintly contemptuous, worth keeping.',
57:'He says 哥 here, clipped, not 哥哥. He does not get the word out. English cannot really clip Brother, so the ellipsis carries it. Throatful of water was my invention. 呛到 is simply choking on it.',
58:'The hardest line here, and it is a tonal pun. Read in second tone the idiom is praise out of the Shishuo Xinyu, two brothers so equal that neither can be ranked above the other. Read in fourth tone it means fellow sufferers, and from there a mocking two of a kind. Han Dian lists all of it, and the Guoyu Cidian says outright that the praise usage later turned derogatory. The jeer activates the insult while the words still literally say brothers. English cannot hold both tones at once. Alternatives still on the table: two of a kind, or a fine pair of brothers played as sarcasm, or brothers in misfortune.',
59:'也没有站出来阻止 quietly indicts him. He has the authority and does not use it. 心中 dropped again, per the rule on 心.',
60:'The title spoken aloud, so it has to match the title word for word. 呢 on the end is a softening particle that makes it almost teasing, which is exactly right given the smile described next.',
61:"玩味 is in Han Dian as turning something over and tasting the flavour in it, English ponder, ruminate, savour, with a citation about reading a passage again and again until it becomes one's own. Playful would be wrong. The smile is not light, it is appetitive. This is the reader's first signal that Fang Yuan has plans for his brother's Grade A aptitude.",
62:'明明 is plainly, and yet, marking a contradiction the boy cannot resolve. Han Dian gives obviously, plainly, undoubtedly. Plainly only was clumsy word order, so obviously takes it. This is thought rather than speech, so elder brother reads naturally, he is thinking about him, not calling to him.',
64:'The clumsiest line in my first pass. 愣愣 is dazed or blank, and hanging it on the noun expression in an English appositive makes it read like a stage direction. Put the state on the boy and it moves again. 花海 stays flower field, per the Chapter 4 ruling, which also spares a second sea in a chapter that already has one.',
65:'上演着一场奇迹式的表演 stacks two theatre words. 上演 is to stage or mount, the word for putting on a play or screening a film, and 表演 is the performance itself, with 奇迹式的 meaning of the miraculous kind. Staging a miracle was tighter, but it threw away the audience, and the audience is the joke: this is a show, with watchers, and the performer is the only person not watching. Restoring performance costs a little speed and buys back the irony.',
66:'惊醒 here is snapping out of a daze, not waking from sleep. The clause about nobody reaching it stays last, it is the payoff.',
67:"Its own paragraph, against Fang Yuan's twenty-seven at L17. That contrast is the structural joke of the chapter.",
68:"失态 is loss of proper bearing by someone expected to keep it. Three exclamation marks are the author's, not mine.",
70:'三年了 with the particle is it has been three years, a span now ended. The again matters, this has happened before.',
71:'大失风范 pairs with 失态 at L68. Everyone dignified is behaving undignifiedly, and the repetition is comic, so both should sit in the same English register.',
72:"脉 is a vein, and by extension any unbroken line: 山脉 a mountain range, 矿脉 a vein of ore, 血脉 a bloodline. Inside a clan it is a branch of descent. Lineage rather than family, because the book runs tiers and family is already taken: 古月族 the clan, 脉 the lineage, 分家 the branch family which turns up later in Chi Lian's study, and 家 the family as in 方家兄弟 at L53. Calling this the Chi Family would collapse two tiers and imply the Chi are separate from the Gu Yue, when the point of the sentence is that they are one clan and Chi Lian is claiming ancestral seniority over a sub-branch. 收养 is formal adoption, a claim of custody. This is a land grab dressed as genealogy.",
73:"Old geezer. I went through old ghost and old wretch before landing here, and both were wrong for reasons worth recording. First the sourcing: CC-CEDICT, the database behind MDBG, Pleco and most dictionary apps, returns no entry at all for 老鬼, so there is no standard bilingual rendering to defer to. Wiktionary is the one bilingual dictionary that glosses it, and it gives derogatory gaffer, geezer for standard Mandarin, plus a separate Shanghainese sense of experienced, smart, astute. The 汉语大词典 entry, carried by several Chinese dictionary sites, says 方言。妻子称丈夫。多作为詈词或谑称, a term of abuse or a jesting appellation, refusing to fix the register. CantoDict has it as an old or experienced person, sometimes used in a cynical friendly way like old pal, and lists 死老鬼 separately as old bastard. That last detail decides the strength: a harsher form existed and the author did not use it, so the word is mid-register and the venom in this line comes from the clauses around it, not from 老鬼 itself. Old wretch therefore over-translated. Second, the form. 鬼 here is a pejorative agentive suffix, not a spirit, and dictionaries never carry it across literally: Han Dian renders 色鬼 as lounge lizard and satyr, not as a ghost of any kind. Old ghost is a calque that keeps a morpheme and loses the meaning, and in this book it carries a specific hazard, since the world actually contains souls and demons and a reader may take it as a description of what Chi Lian is. Geezer costs some period flavour and reads colloquial, but it behaves the way 老鬼 behaves, taking its force from context, and the scene is comic anyway: every elder present has lost his dignity and the clan leader is about to go into a frenzy. On the man himself, checked in the book rather than guessed: Chapter 4 gives Chi Lian 一头红发, a head of red hair, and calls him the 爷爷 of Gu Yue Chi Cheng, and in Chapter 21 he calls himself 爷爷. He is a man and a grandfather, so old bag and old hag are out as fact rather than taste. Chapter 4 also makes the two elders 老对头, long standing enemies heading the clan's two largest factions with a grandson each in this ceremony, and establishes that Chi Lian falsified his own grandson's aptitude, which makes the jab accidentally true. Hamper is Han Dian's own English for 误人子弟. 何德何能 is normally self-deprecating, so turning it outward is what makes it a sneer. And 触电 has a figurative sense in Han Dian, 比喻突然受到强烈的刺激或震撼, a sudden violent stimulus, so it describes the onset of his reaction, not the roaring.",
74:"Rewritten. My first version had four separate faults. One, mood: 谁都别争了 is an imperative, 别 being don't and 了 marking the change of state, so it is stop this, not a prediction. None of you will contend for him turned an order into a prophecy, which also made him sound serenely in control when the last clause says he is in a frenzy. Two, 栽培 does repeat in the Chinese, 谁栽培 then 亲自栽培的好, but grooms followed by grooming is clumsy in English, and do it carries the second instance without the stumble. Three, 异议 and 反对 are two different words, dissent and oppose, and I collapsed them into objects and objects, inventing a repetition the author did not write while losing the one he did. Four, gone entirely into a frenzy was limp. 本族长 stays as this clan leader, since referring to himself by office is the authority claim the sentence is built on. 栽培 is Han Dian's plant and tend, extended to training and fostering talent and then to patronage in old officialdom, which is why groom rather than raise; L73 is 抚养, ordinary childrearing. And 癫狂 is a named disorder in Chinese medicine, derangement, plus a colloquial sense of being beside oneself. Both are live here, and frenzy holds them.",
}